        <div class="qa-box"><span class="qa-label">Quick answer</span><p>The <strong>Power BI service</strong> is Microsoft's cloud platform for publishing, sharing and refreshing Power BI reports. <strong>Microsoft Power Platform</strong> is the larger family that includes Power BI plus <strong>Power Apps</strong> (custom apps), <strong>Power Automate</strong> (workflows), <strong>Copilot Studio</strong> and <strong>Dataverse</strong>. Most small businesses should start with Power BI Desktop and the Power BI service for reporting, and add the other Power Platform tools later.</p></div>
        <p class="updated-note">Last updated November 2026 · Licensing and prices change, so confirm current details with Microsoft</p>

        <p>Search for "power bi service" or "power platform" and you will find a thicket of product names, licenses and diagrams. For a small business owner trying to decide what to actually buy, the confusion is understandable. This guide separates the pieces in plain English and explains what each does, how they relate, and what a business with a handful of employees really needs.</p>
        <p>If you want the business case for dashboards first, read our guide to <a href="power-bi-consulting-for-small-business.html">Power BI consulting for small business</a>. For a hands-on walkthrough, see <a href="how-to-build-a-business-dashboard-in-power-bi.html">how to build a business dashboard in Power BI</a>.</p>

        <h2 id="the-pieces">The pieces: Desktop, Service and Platform</h2>
        <p>Three terms get mixed up constantly, so here they are side by side.</p>
        <div class="compare-wrap"><table class="compare">
          <tr><th>Name</th><th>What it is</th><th>Who uses it</th></tr>
          <tr><td>Power BI Desktop</td><td>A free Windows application for connecting to data, cleaning it and building reports</td><td>The person who builds the reports</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Power BI service</td><td>The cloud service where reports are published, shared, refreshed and viewed</td><td>Builders and viewers</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Power Platform</td><td>The wider family of low-code tools that includes Power BI, Power Apps, Power Automate, Copilot Studio and Dataverse</td><td>Anyone building apps, automations or analytics</td></tr>
        </table></div>
        <p>A simple way to remember it: <strong>Desktop builds, the Service shares, and the Platform extends.</strong></p>

        <h2 id="power-bi-service">What the Power BI service does</h2>
        <p>Once you build a report in Power BI Desktop, it lives on your computer until you publish it. The Power BI service is where it goes next. Typical uses include:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Sharing:</strong> giving colleagues, managers or clients access through a browser or mobile app.</li>
          <li><strong>Scheduled refresh:</strong> updating the data automatically so dashboards stay current.</li>
          <li><strong>Collaboration:</strong> shared workspaces where a team manages reports together.</li>
          <li><strong>Dashboards and alerts:</strong> pinning key tiles into one view and getting notified when a number crosses a threshold.</li>
          <li><strong>Access control:</strong> deciding who can see which reports and data.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>If a dashboard is for you alone, Desktop may be enough. If anyone else needs to see it, you will use the service.</p>

        <h2 id="licensing">Power BI licensing in plain English</h2>
        <p>Licensing is where most small businesses get tripped up. In broad terms:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Free:</strong> you can build reports in Desktop and publish to your personal workspace, but you generally cannot share with other people.</li>
          <li><strong>Pro:</strong> a per-user license that lets you share and collaborate in shared workspaces. Typically, both the person sharing and the person viewing need a Pro license.</li>
          <li><strong>Premium Per User:</strong> a higher per-user tier with more advanced features.</li>
          <li><strong>Premium capacity:</strong> a capacity-based option aimed at larger organizations, where many viewers can access content without individual Pro licenses.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Published prices change often, and third-party sources can be out of date, so check Microsoft's current pricing page before you plan a budget. The key question to ask is simple: <strong>how many people need to view or edit reports?</strong> That number, more than your company size, drives your license cost. For a view of what consulting adds on top, see our breakdown of <a href="power-bi-consulting-cost.html">Power BI consulting cost</a>.</p>

        <h2 id="power-platform">What is Microsoft Power Platform?</h2>
        <p>Microsoft describes Power Platform as a low-code, rapid business application development platform containing several independent but closely related tools. The main components are:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Power BI:</strong> a self-service analytics service that lets people gain insights from their data.</li>
          <li><strong>Power Apps:</strong> lets people build custom web and mobile apps with low-code or no-code techniques.</li>
          <li><strong>Power Automate:</strong> a cloud workflow tool that connects cloud and desktop applications to automate business processes.</li>
          <li><strong>Copilot Studio:</strong> lets people create copilot agents without writing code.</li>
          <li><strong>Microsoft Dataverse:</strong> a no-code environment for creating tables, relationships and business logic, described by Microsoft as more than a database.</li>
          <li><strong>Connectors:</strong> the links to the services and data sources that the tools can access.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Microsoft's documentation also lists AI Builder and Power Pages among related tools. The important point is that the pieces share data and connectors, so work you do in one can feed the others.</p>

        <h2 id="examples">What each tool looks like in a small business</h2>
        <p><strong>Power BI:</strong> a dashboard that combines ad spend, sales and inventory for an e-commerce brand, so a founder sees what to reorder and which campaigns pay off.</p>
        <p><strong>Power Automate:</strong> when a new lead arrives in a form, create a record in your CRM, send a notification and add a task, without anyone copying data by hand.</p>
        <p><strong>Power Apps:</strong> a simple internal app for field staff to log jobs or inspections from a phone, replacing a messy spreadsheet.</p>
        <p><strong>Dataverse:</strong> a structured place to store the data those apps use, once a spreadsheet becomes unmanageable.</p>

        <h2 id="what-to-buy-first">What a small business should buy first</h2>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Start with Power BI Desktop.</strong> It is free, so you can prototype your first dashboard before spending anything.</li>
          <li><strong>Count your viewers.</strong> If only you and one or two colleagues need access, per-user licensing is usually the simplest path.</li>
          <li><strong>Add Power Automate</strong> when you notice repetitive copy-and-paste tasks eating hours every week.</li>
          <li><strong>Add Power Apps</strong> when a spreadsheet is being used as a makeshift application and keeps breaking.</li>
          <li><strong>Consider Dataverse and larger capacity</strong> only when volume, security or governance requires it.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>The mistake to avoid is buying the full platform before you have a clear problem. Each tool should earn its place by saving time or improving a decision.</p>

        <h2 id="security">Security and sharing basics</h2>
        <p>Dashboards often contain customer, sales and financial data, so decide who sees what before you publish. Use workspaces to group reports by team, give people the lowest level of access they need, and avoid public sharing options for anything sensitive. If different people should see different rows of the same report, such as a regional manager seeing only their region, ask about row-level security during setup. Keep a short record of who owns each report and who approved access, and review it every quarter. If you serve customers in California or the EU, check with a qualified professional that your reporting practices fit laws such as CCPA and GDPR.</p>

        <h2 id="mistakes">Common mistakes</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Assuming Desktop is enough</strong> when others need to view reports. Sharing needs the service and appropriate licenses.</li>
          <li><strong>Publishing to the web</strong> for private data. Publicly accessible reports are unsuitable for sensitive business information.</li>
          <li><strong>Building around too many tools at once.</strong> Get one dashboard right first.</li>
          <li><strong>Skipping documentation.</strong> If only one person understands the model, you are exposed when they leave.</li>
          <li><strong>Ignoring data governance.</strong> Decide who can see what, especially customer and financial data.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 id="diy-or-consultant">Build it yourself or hire a consultant?</h2>
        <p>If your data sits in one or two clean sources and you have time to learn, you can build a useful first dashboard yourself. If data is scattered across your CRM, ad platforms, accounting software and store, or you need something reliable quickly, a consultant usually saves time and avoids rework. When comparing <strong>Power BI consulting services</strong>, ask about scope, documentation, training for your team, and what happens after launch.</p>

        <h2 id="our-approach">How Tycoon Clap helps</h2>
        <p>We set up Power BI for small and mid-sized businesses by starting with the decisions you make, connecting your real data sources, defining metrics once and building a focused set of dashboards. We handle publishing and licensing guidance, then train your team to run it. When you are ready for automation or internal apps, we can extend the same data foundation into Power Automate and Power Apps. Book a strategy call to talk through what you actually need.</p>

        <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>
        <div class="article-faq">
          <details open><summary>What is the Power BI service?</summary><p class="faq-a">The Power BI service is Microsoft's cloud service where Power BI reports and dashboards are published, shared, refreshed and viewed in a browser. You typically build reports in Power BI Desktop, then publish them to the service so other people can use them.</p></details>
          <details><summary>What is Microsoft Power Platform?</summary><p class="faq-a">Microsoft describes Power Platform as a low-code, rapid business application development platform made up of closely related tools: Power Apps for custom apps, Power Automate for workflows, Power BI for analytics, Copilot Studio for building copilot agents, and Microsoft Dataverse for data, plus data connectors.</p></details>
          <details><summary>Is Power BI part of Power Platform?</summary><p class="faq-a">Yes. Power BI is the analytics and reporting component of Microsoft Power Platform, alongside Power Apps, Power Automate and Copilot Studio. You can use Power BI on its own without the other tools.</p></details>
          <details><summary>Is Power BI Desktop free?</summary><p class="faq-a">Yes, Power BI Desktop is free to download and use for building reports. Sharing reports with colleagues through the Power BI service generally requires paid licenses, and prices and plans change, so check Microsoft's current licensing before you commit.</p></details>
          <details><summary>Do I need Power Apps and Power Automate if I use Power BI?</summary><p class="faq-a">Not at the start. Most small businesses should begin with Power BI for reporting, then add Power Automate to remove repetitive tasks and Power Apps for simple internal tools once the reporting foundation is working.</p></details>
          <details><summary>Should I hire a Power BI consultant?</summary><p class="faq-a">A consultant makes sense when your data is spread across several systems, metric definitions are disputed, or you need a dashboard built quickly and correctly. A good consultant scopes narrowly, documents the work and trains your team to maintain it.</p></details>
        </div><p style="font-size:.88rem;color:var(--ink-dim);line-height:1.7;margin-top:2rem;">Power Platform component descriptions summarized from Microsoft Learn. Licensing details change frequently; confirm current plans and prices with Microsoft. Tycoon Clap is not affiliated with Microsoft.</p>
